<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 256–336</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arius was a Christian priest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> whose teaching that the Son was created by God the Father, and subordinate to him, caused a long controversy in the church. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">Council of Nicaea</a> condemned his views in 325, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> remained influential for decades and was adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>. His chief opponent was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athanasius-of-alexandria">Athanasius of Alexandria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arius.jpg" alt="Arius" width="320" height="158" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Damaskinos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ariusz.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Reconstructing the life and doctrine of Arius has proved difficult. He was of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> descent, and his father's name is given as Ammonius. In 313 he became presbyter of the Baucalis district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. His opponents attacked his character, but he appears to have been a man of personal ascetic achievement, pure morals and decided convictions. Modern scholars including Williams, Hanson and Lewis Ayres hold that he was not the founder of a new sect but a conservative presbyter who gave voice to a doctrinal crisis already gathering before his dispute with Bishop Alexander of Alexandria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Arian controversy began in 318, when Arius publicly criticised Alexander for blurring the distinction between Father and Son. According to the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, he argued that if the Father begat the Son, the Son had a beginning, and that there was a time when the Son was not. Alexander exiled him after a council of local priests, but Arius appealed to the Eastern church and found support from Eusebius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> and Eusebius of Caesarea. The dispute spread beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, and in 325 Constantine called a council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, which condemned his teaching and exiled him.</p><p>The controversy resumed at once. Athanasius succeeded Alexander in 327. Constantine grew more lenient and allowed Arius, who had taken refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>, to return once he had reformulated his Christology. The Synod of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> restored him to communion in 336. Constantine, baptised on his deathbed in 337 by Eusebius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a>, never repudiated the council. Arius died in 336.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Arius held that the Son was not coeternal with the Father but was created by him before anything else. He emphasised the supremacy and uniqueness of the Father, and held that the Son was made God only by the Father's permission and power. Little of his writing survives: three letters and fragments of the Thalia, a work combining prose and verse, quoted by his opponents, mostly Athanasius, so that scholars question their reliability. Hanson thought so little survived because his contemporaries did not regard him as a particularly significant writer. Arian churches persisted for centuries among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and elsewhere, until they were suppressed between the fifth and seventh centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 210 BC Antiochus III attacked Euthydemus, who commanded 10,000 horsemen, lost a battle on the <b>Arius</b> and then held Bactra through a three-year siege until Antiochus recognised him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Athanasius, bishop of Alexandria from 328 to 373, defended the creed of the Council of Nicaea against the followers of <b>Arius</b>, an Alexandrian priest, and his Life of Antony carried the monastic ideal west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.28,21.63&amp;year=308" aria-label="Cyrenaica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5106 990 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Arius"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5556 1260h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5556 1260h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.28,21.63&amp;year=308">What was here in 308 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
